latest_plan = 'Based on the reflection, here\'s a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze Input Grid:\n   a. Count the frequency of each color.\n   b. Identify the top 4-10 most frequent colors (depending on input complexity).\n   c. Calculate the overall complexity of the input (e.g., number of color changes, distribution of colors).\n\n2. Determine Output Size:\n   a. Based on input complexity, choose an output size between 4x4 and 10x10.\n   b. More complex inputs should result in larger output grids.\n\n3. Create Color Importance Map:\n   a. Assign importance scores to colors based on frequency and positioning in the input grid.\n   b. Create a ranked list of colors from most to least important.\n\n4. Analyze Color Relationships:\n   a. For each pair of important colors, calculate how often they appear adjacent to each other.\n   b. Create a relationship matrix showing which colors tend to appear near each other.\n\n5. Determine Overall Structure:\n   a. Analyze the distribution of colors across the input grid (e.g., are certain colors more prevalent in certain areas?).\n   b. Determine if there\'s any overall symmetry or directionality to the color distribution.\n\n6. Create Initial Output Grid:\n   a. Divide the output grid into regions based on the color importance map.\n   b. Assign the most important colors to the largest regions, maintaining their relative positions from the input if possible.\n\n7. Refine Color Mapping:\n   a. For each region in the output grid, analyze the corresponding area in the input grid.\n   b. Choose a representative color for each region based on the most frequent color in that area of the input.\n   c. Adjust colors to maintain relationships identified in step 4.\n\n8. Balance Color Distribution:\n   a. Calculate the percentage of each important color in the output grid.\n   b. Compare this to the color distribution in the input grid.\n   c. Make small adjustments to the output grid to better match the input distribution.\n\n9. Enhance Contrast:\n   a. Identify adjacent regions in the output grid with similar colors.\n   b. If possible, adjust one of these regions to a contrasting color that was also present in the input.\n\n10. Final Abstraction:\n    a. For very small output grids (e.g., 4x4), consider further abstraction by choosing only the most representative colors.\n    b. Ensure that the final output captures the "feel" of the input rather than specific details.\n\n11. Validate Output:\n    a. Check that the output grid size is within the required range (4x4 to 10x10).\n    b. Verify that all colors in the output were present in the input.\n    c. Ensure that the output maintains the general color balance and structure of the input.\n\nThis plan aims to create an abstracted representation of the input that captures its essence in terms of color importance, relationships, and overall structure, without trying to preserve specific patterns or shapes. It should be flexible enough to handle inputs of varying complexity and produce outputs that range from highly abstracted (like in Example 3) to more detailed representations (like in Example 2), depending on the input characteristics.'
